I thought it might be interesting to do the numbers on this. I eagerly
await the response pointing out my arithmetic error.

+3 armour has a 1/2 chance of becoming either +4 or +5.
+2 armour has a 1/3 chance of becoming +3, +4, or +5.
+1 armour has a 1/3 chance of becoming +2, +3, or +4.
+0 armour has a 1/3 chance of becoming +1, +2, or +3.

Let scroll(+n) represent the expected total scroll usage when starting at
+n and armour(+n) be the expected armour enchantment when starting at +n.

If I do not plan on using drain life;

Enchantment; Scroll use;
+3           1
+2           1+(1/3 scroll(+3))
             = 4/3
+1           1+(1/3 scroll(+2) + 1/3 scroll(+3)) =
             1+4/9+1/3 = 9/9+4/9+3/9 
             = 16/9
+0           1+(1/3 scroll(+1) + 1/3 scroll(+2) + 1/3 scroll(+3)) =
             1+(16/27 + 4/9 + 1/3) = 
             27/27+(16/27 + 12/27 + 9/27) = 
             64/27
wished +5    4/5 scroll(+0) = 256/134
             =~ 1.90 scrolls
wished +4    3/5 scroll(+0) = 192/135
             =~ 1.42 scrolls
wished +3    3/5 scroll(+3) + 2/5 scroll(+0) =
             3/5 + 128/135 = 81/135 + 128/135 = 209/135
             =~ 1.55 scrolls
wished +2    4/5 scroll(+2) + 1/5 scroll(+0) =
             16/15 + 64/135 = 144/135+64/135 = 208/135
             =~ 1.54 scrolls
wished +1    16/9
             =~ 1.78 scrolls

Enchantment; Eventual enchantment;
+3           +4.5
+2           (1/3 * 4) + (1/3 * 5) + (1/3 * armour(+3)) = +4.5
+1           (1/3 * 4) + (1/3 armour(+2) + 1/3 armour (+3)) = 
             4+ (1/6 + 1/6) = +(4 1/3)
+0           1/3 armour(+1) + 1/3 armour (+2) + 1/3 armour (+3)
             = 4+ (1/9 + 1/6 + 1/6)
             = +(4 4/9)
wished +5    1 + 4/5 armour (+0)
             1 + 160/45 = 205/45
             =~ +4.556
wished +4    8/5 + 3/5 armour (+0)
             = 4+ 12/45
             =~ +4.267
wished +3    3/5 armour (+3) + 2/5 armour (+0)
             = 4+ (3/10 + 8/45) = 4 + (27/90 + 16/90) = +4 (43/90)
             =~ +4.478
wished +2    4/5 armour (+2) + 1/5 armour (+0)
             = 4+ (4/10 + 4/45) = 4+(36/90 + 8/90) = 4+(44/90)
             =~ +4.489
wished +1    ~= +4.333

In other words, if you will not use drain life, your expected scroll
consumption and eventual armour class are better for +2, but only
extremely marginally so. Wishing for +4 minimises scroll consumption but
costs AC; wishing for +5 maximises AC but costs scrolls and is risky in
the case where I won't be doing the enchantment right away, because the
expected gain in AC from the wish itself is small.

If I do plan on using drain life;

Enchantment; Scroll use;
+3           2 ( = 1+1/2+1/4+1/8+ ...)
+2           1+(2/3 scroll(+3))
             = 7/3
+1           1+(1/3 scroll(+2) + 2/3 scroll(+3)) =
             1+7/9+4/3 = 9/9+7/9+12/9 
             = 28/9
+0           1+(1/3 scroll(+1) + 1/3 scroll(+2) + 1/3 scroll(+3)) =
             1+(28/27 + 7/9 + 2/3) = 
             27/27+(28/27 + 21/27 + 18/27) = 
             94/27
wished +5    4/5 scroll(+0) = 376/135
             =~ 2.79 scrolls
wished +4    obviously braindead
wished +3    3/5 scroll(+3) + 2/5 scroll(+0) =
             6/5 + 188/135 = 162/135 + 188/135
             = 350/135 =~ 2.59 scrolls
wished +2    4/5 scroll(+2) + 1/5 scroll(+0) =
             28/15 + 94/135 = 252/135+94/135
             346/135 =~ 2.56 scrolls
wished +1    28/9 =~ 3.11 scrolls

Manifestly the expected eventual enchantment is +5.

Hence if you will use drain life your expected scroll consumption is still
marginally lower with +2, and +5 and +1 are obviously useless options.

However, the analysis for the latter case neglects to consider the
non-zero possibility that you will run out of scrolls and marker charges.
I would not be entirely surprised if there are some specific numbers of
available scrolls where wishing for +3 offers a greater expected outcome,
and some numbers of remaining scrolls where it is best to abandon the
drain-lifing strategy.

[Indeed, even using the former strategy you may have insufficient scrolls,
but at least the scroll use per item has an upper maximum, 4.]
